Covee: a device that signs in again after its key expired reaches its peers

When a device's key expires, peers are sent the whole node with Expired set
and MachineAuthorized cleared, and Tailscale clients keep both. A same-user
relogin was then announced only as a key-rotation peer patch
(change.NodeKeyRotated). tailcfg.PeerChange has no field for Expired or
MachineAuthorized, so peers that stayed connected kept treating the device as
expired: the route manager gives an expired peer no AllowedIPs, and its
traffic was dropped both ways until each peer reconnected. A relogin of a
node that had expired is now a whole-node add, as SetNodeExpiry already does
when it un-expires a node. A relogin of a node that had not expired keeps the
patch. Both sign-in paths (web/OIDC and pre-auth key) are covered.

TestCoveeExpiredReloginClearsExpiredForPeers reproduces it with real control
clients, for a key expired on the server and for logout then login; it fails
without this change (the peer keeps Expired=true MachineAuthorized=false).

